licated and what we do what we  should do is just focus on one of these  bars so let's look at 2018 and break it  down it's about 78 billion in total but  of that okay we'll take that 78 billion  of that a good chunk is the private  sector rather than the public sector so  companies giving money to poorer  countries some as export credits as well  and if we drill into that public sector  bit specifically of that 61.6 billion  the vast majority 47 billion or so is  loans so it's not giving money it's  actually lending money and expecting it  to be repaid fair enough but it's  slightly different from what people  describe this typically as and of that  total you might be wondering how much is  the uk well it's that red slice 1.6  billion dollars or so it's actually a  little bit less than you might have  expected which is strange given the uk  is encouraging other countries to pay  more  well let's take a look at some of the  other day's news now from cop 26 and of  course around the world the prime  minister has returned to westminster and  has been reflecting on whether cop 26  will be successful in keeping the  warming target of 1.5 degrees alive or  boris johnson told mps that words still  need to be turned into action to avoid  climate disaster  in the end mr speaker it is a question  of will  we have the technology to do what is  necessary  all that remains in question is our  resolve  the negotiations in glasgow have almost  two weeks to run  but we can take heart from what has been  achieved so far  well mclaren has unveiled its new  extreme e-car at cop26 there it is  prince charles was one of the first to  see the british-based team's offering  for the off-road electric race series  which aims to raise awareness about  climate change by racing in environments  that are already suffering from the  effects of global warming  he's been busy prince charles has also  been dipping his toe in the sustainable  fashion world teaming up with stella  mccartney and leon