e a sudden uh  killing of an industry can you you have  to sort of unwind it gradually what do  you think about that  so  the this 130 trillion needs to be guided  by  transition plans with regular reporting  of progress and that needs to be put on  a regulatory footing and the uk is the  first to to do that  and in addition to that of course we  need to make sure that the real economy  the changes that are happening around  the world  people that are affected companies are  affected get the capital they need get  the support they need and one thing we  need to do is to convert polluting  companies into clean ones and they're  going to need capital to do that so we  need financial markets to provide them  with the resources they need to  transition as well the other pots of  money that of course we've been talking  about is that hundred billion dollars a  year that was pledged in 2009 in  copenhagen looks like i mean we're  certainly going to fall short talk of it  being met in 2023 have you seen  promising signs around that felicia very  much so i mean there have been so  there's been so many different  announcements that it was a huge problem  when it was announced that it wasn't  going to be met because obviously that  undermines the trust in the negotiations  people's ability exactly but with some  of the announcements we've seen in the  last couple of days you know japan  a number of companies sorry countries  have actually said they're going to  contribute larger amounts of climate  finance i think the key thing is the  overall transition because 100 billion  is a drop in the ocean in terms of the  amount of money that's actually needed  what the 100 billion is about is public  commitment from developing countries to  say you know we're asking you to take  action and we're going to stand up to  the plate and provide you with the funds  and the support and the capacity  building that you need to do a just  transition because as ben says you know  you have to be